## Layout

`data/raw/` is immutable and gitignored — treat it read-only, never edit it
in place. `data/interim/` and `data/processed/` are also gitignored;
regenerate both from `data/raw/` via `src/{{ project_slug }}/`, don't hand-edit
or commit them. `notebooks/` stays exploratory; anything worth reusing gets
promoted into `src/{{ project_slug }}/` as a tested function. `reports/figures/`
holds the actual deliverables — those aren't gitignored.

## Notebooks

- Ruff lints and formats `.ipynb` natively — `just format`/`just
  check-ruff`/`just check-format` already cover `notebooks/`, no extra
  tooling needed.
- `mypy`/`complexipy`/`pytest` don't touch notebooks — `mypy` is told to
  skip `notebooks/`/`data/`/`reports/` explicitly (`[tool.mypy] exclude`
  in `pyproject.toml`); `complexipy`/`pytest` never look at non-`.py`
  content anyway. Deliberate, not an oversight: notebooks stay
  exploratory, code worth type-checking/testing belongs in `src/`.
- `just clean-notebooks` strips cell outputs/execution counts (also wired
  in as a prek hook, runs automatically pre-commit) — keeps diffs to
  actual code changes and stops `codespell` choking on base64 image blobs.
- Runtime: **{{ notebook_runtime }}**, chosen at generation time.
  - `ipykernel`: use VS Code's Jupyter extension, pick this project's
    `.venv` as the kernel — no separate kernel registration needed.
  - `jupyterlab`: `just lab` launches JupyterLab with Ruff wired in as a
    live LSP (lint/format-on-save in the browser editor) via the
    project-local `jupyter_server_config.json` — picked up automatically
    since JupyterLab reads it from the working directory. Live in-browser
    `mypy` is deliberately not wired in (would need a second LSP server,
    `pylsp` + `pylsp-mypy`, for feedback that arguably shouldn't apply to
    throwaway exploration cells anyway) — run `just check-types` instead.
  - `both`/`none`: as above, or bring your own.

## Setup

```
just install
```

`codespell` and `prek` install via `just install`. `gitleaks` is a
standalone binary, install separately, pinned to match `prek.toml`:

```bash
GITLEAKS_VERSION={{ gitleaks_version }}
curl -sSL -o gitleaks.tar.gz "https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${GITLEAKS_VERSION}/gitleaks_${GITLEAKS_VERSION}_linux_x64.tar.gz"
tar xzf gitleaks.tar.gz gitleaks
sudo mv gitleaks /usr/local/bin/
rm gitleaks.tar.gz
gitleaks version
```

If you use VS Code, install the Python extension alongside the Ruff extension — otherwise Ruff-in-editor can silently fall back to a PATH/bundled binary instead of the `uv.lock`-pinned one in your venv.

## Git hooks

```
just install-hooks    # one-time: installs prek's git hook shims (see prek.toml)
```

Runs formatting, type checks, and complexity checks automatically before
each commit, plus commit message / branch-name conventions (commitizen),
generic file hygiene, spell checking, and secret scanning. Install this
once per clone.

## Dev commands

```
just format           # ruff check --fix + ruff format (covers notebooks/ too)
just check-format     # ruff format --check (non-mutating)
just check-types      # mypy (notebooks/data/reports excluded, see pyproject.toml)
just check-complexity # complexipy
just check-spelling      # codespell
just check-secrets       # gitleaks, staged changes only — run before committing
just check-versions-sync # gitleaks pin in prek.toml vs. installed binary
just clean-notebooks     # strip notebook outputs/execution counts
just lab                 # launch JupyterLab (ruff wired in as LSP)
just test                # pytest + coverage
just check               # format + types + complexity + spelling + version-sync + test bundle
```

`check-secrets` is deliberately not part of the `just check` bundle — it
only makes sense once something's staged. Run it (or rely on the
`install-hooks` git hook) before every commit.

`codespell`, like `ruff`/`mypy`/`complexipy`, is a venv dependency — its
pre-commit hook just runs `just check-spelling`, so there's a single pin
(`pyproject.toml`) and nothing to drift. `gitleaks` is a standalone binary
outside the venv, pinned separately in `prek.toml` and the README install
snippet above; `just check-versions-sync` (also wired in as a prek hook)
checks the installed binary against that pin so the two can't silently
drift apart.
